---
// /use-cases/web-scraping (2026-07-03, S14 / D8) — persona page for
// data teams. Claim register: homepage §4 card ("See what iPhone users
// see" / mobile-vs-desktop divergence / no please-use-our-app
// redirects), §6 proof section ("One iPhone among millions", costume
// metaphor, population-stable hashes — reused verbatim in spirit, no
// new percentages or scale claims), §8 SDK trio. AUP discipline: the
// scraping boundary is named plainly with the /legal/aup link —
// capability description, never encouragement.
// 2026-09-15 truth pass: the ships-on-the-iPhone browser line → "a build of
// Apple's own WebKit — the engine family behind iPhone Safari"; "the real iPhone
// value on every one of them" → "checked against real devices, signal by
// signal" (81/96 device profiles byte-identical, not a blanket identity);
// the stability Stat now says which answers are fixed and which Safari
// deliberately varies (canvas/audio carry per-read noise on a real iPhone
// too); clock + time zone follow the exit, language is not claimed.
// 2026-09-16 readability pass: pure Band A. Section leads open with what the
// reader gets, sentences carry one idea each, and the user-agent/Chromium
// vocabulary is stated in plain words. The Stat small print (Band B) keeps its
// exact technical lines.
// 2026-09-16 (verifier fixes, same pass):
//   • The "Which plan" lead names the BILLING number and no longer reads as an
//     exclusivity claim about plan limits. Every API tier also caps stored
//     profiles (data/pricing.ts: 25 / 100 / 500, held to the live schema by
//     W279.A and columned on /pricing), so concurrency is not the only limit.
//   • SDK / HTTP client / webhooks left the lead, which Section renders as
//     plain text and so cannot carry a link. They now live in body paragraphs
//     where SDK links to /glossary/#sdk and webhooks are glossed in plain words.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import Stat from '../../components/Stat.astro';
import FaqList from '../../components/FaqList.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { FAQ_GROUPS } from '../../data/faq';

// The two FAQ groups data teams read before switching: how migration
// from the Chromium vendors works, and where the acceptable-use line is.
const faqGroups = FAQ_GROUPS.filter((g) =>
  ['Migrating from another vendor', 'Acceptable use'].includes(g.title),
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Mobile web data collection"
  description="Many sites serve mobile Safari different content than desktop Chrome. Driftstack collectors are real iPhone Safari sessions, so you gather what an iPhone visitor actually sees."
  ogImage="/og/use-cases.png"
>
  <PageHero
    label="Use cases · Data teams"
    title="See what iPhone users see."
    lead="Many sites serve mobile Safari a different site than desktop Chrome: different layouts, different flows, different content. If your collector isn't an iPhone, you're not collecting what iPhone users get."
  >
    <Breadcrumbs
      slot="crumbs"
      class="mb-5"
      items={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'Use cases', href: '/use-cases/' }, { name: 'Web scraping' }]}
    />
  </PageHero>

  <Section
    label="The problem"
    title="The mobile web is a different web."
  >
    <div class="mt-8 max-w-prose space-y-5 text-base leading-7 text-tk-ink-2">
      <p>
        Point a desktop collector at a mobile-first site and you get the
        desktop version. Or worse, a full-screen "please use our app"
        roadblock instead of the page. Change the browser's name so it claims
        to be a phone and you hit the next problem: detection. Websites have
        gotten very good at spotting a desktop browser in a phone costume.
        The disguise only covers the details the stealth tool remembered to
        fake.
      </p>
      <p>
        Driftstack doesn't wear a costume. Every session runs a build of
        Apple's own WebKit — the engine family behind iPhone Safari. So
        your collector sees what an iPhone visitor would see: the
        mobile layout, the mobile flow, no app-steering redirects.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    label="Why it holds up"
    title="One iPhone among millions."
    lead="Your session looks like one of millions of real iPhones. Websites read hundreds of small signals. Driftstack gives the answers a real iPhone gives, checked against real devices, signal by signal."
  >
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <Stat
        plain="Looks human to every website"
        technical="The hidden checks websites run to spot fakes get the answers a real iPhone gives — checked against real devices, check by check"
      />
      <Stat
        plain="Stable where a real iPhone is stable"
        technical="The fixed answers — screen, hardware, audio settings — stay fixed and match what millions of real iPhones share, and the ones Safari deliberately varies are varied the way a real iPhone varies them, session after session; disguised desktop browsers produce a brand-new value every session — 100% unique, which is itself a giveaway"
      />
      <Stat
        plain="Any country you need"
        technical="Bring your own proxy or VPN, attached per profile — the phone's clock and time zone follow that location, so nothing contradicts the address a website sees"
      />
    </div>
    <p class="mt-8 max-w-prose text-sm leading-6 text-tk-ink-3">
      Want the detail? The
      <a href="/comparison/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-tk-accent-2">comparison page</a>
      goes signal by signal: what a detector measures, and what each side
      returns.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    label="Which plan"
    title="Collectors are code — pick the API plan family."
    lead="Collectors are written in code, so the API plans are the ones built for them. Billing turns on one number: how many sessions run at the same time — think browser tabs. Hours inside that number are never billed."
  >
    <p class="mt-8 max-w-prose text-base leading-7 text-tk-ink-2">
      Your own code drives the sessions. We publish an
      <a href="/glossary/#sdk" class="text-tk-accent-text underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-tk-accent-2">SDK</a>
      — a ready-made code library — for TypeScript, Python and Go. Any program
      that can make an HTTPS request can drive a session too. A session takes a
      short list of actions: navigate, interact, capture, extract.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-base leading-7 text-tk-ink-2">
      The AI agent is on every API plan. Webhooks are messages Driftstack sends
      to your own system. They tell your job when a session completes, fails, or
      hits a challenge.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
      <a href="/pricing/#api" class="btn-secondary">See API pricing →</a>
      <p class="text-sm text-tk-ink-3">
        Switching from a Chrome-based tool? The FAQ below covers what changes.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-8 max-w-prose text-sm leading-6 text-tk-ink-3">
      Collect responsibly. This page is about market research and price
      monitoring. Scraping that gets around logins
      (authentication) or past a site's reasonable rate limits is not
      allowed. The
      <a href="/legal/aup/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-tk-accent-2">Acceptable Use Policy</a>
      draws that line in plain words.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    label="Questions data teams ask"
    title="Before you switch — asked and answered."
  >
    <div class="mt-10 space-y-12">
      {
        faqGroups.map((group) => (
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink">{group.title}</h3>
            <FaqList entries={group.entries} class="mt-5" />
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-tk-ink-3">
      Everything else is on the
      <a href="/faq/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-tk-accent-2">full FAQ</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <CtaBand
    title="Collect the web your users actually see."
    lead="Run a free session by hand, next to your current vendor, and compare what each one collects on your own pages. No card required."
    primaryHref="/pricing/#free"
    primaryLabel="Start free"
    secondaryHref="/pricing/#api"
    secondaryLabel="See API pricing"
  />
</BaseLayout>
